{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-14780",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For this integral, we choose $u=4{x}^{4}+2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4{x}^{4}+2$ | ${e}^{x}$ |  |  |\n| $16{x}^{3}$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $(4{x}^{4}+2){e}^{x}$ |\n| $48{x}^{2}$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $-$ | $-16{x}^{3}{e}^{x}$ |\n| $96x$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $48{x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ |\n| $96$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $-$ | $-96x{e}^{x}$ |\n| $0$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $96{e}^{x}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{4}+2){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}(4{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}+48{x}^{2}-96x+98)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
